Load-bearing rules:
1. Indent = `level` repetitions of the 4-space string literal "    ". Never '\t' (the source file is tab-indented, which makes an accidental tab easy). Emitted BEFORE the label in both branches. main() calls preOrder(0), so the subtree root sits at column 0.
2. LEAF branch prints ONLY `info` — the bare relation name (EMPLOYEE, DEPARTMENT, WORKS_ON, PROJECT). No "LEAF" keyword, no brackets, no punctuation.
3. Operator branch prints `type`, then a SINGLE HARD-CODED SPACE, then `info`. No brackets anywhere: `SELECT ENAME='Mary'`, `PROJECTION BDATE`, `PROJECTION ESSN,PNAME`.
4. CRITICAL: the space in the operator branch is UNCONDITIONAL. JOIN nodes have info == "", so every JOIN line is `JOIN ` WITH A TRAILING SPACE (6 such lines in the transcript). Do NOT "clean this up" with an `info.empty()` guard and do NOT print `JOIN [  ]`.
5. Exactly one line per node, terminated by std::endl. preOrder emits no blank lines, no connectors (`->`, `|--`), no counters, no Greek symbols. All blank lines come from main().
6. Recursion order: current node, then left, then right, both at level + 1, each NULL-guarded. A JOIN's two leaves therefore print at equal indent, in parser order (EMPLOYEE before DEPARTMENT, WORKS_ON before PROJECT).
7. The artificial root is never printed: main() calls root->left->preOrder(0).
8. Info strings are SPACE-FREE, because the reference parseQuery concatenates the tokens between `[` and `]` with no separator: `ENAME='Mary'&DNAME='Research'`, `ESSN='01'`, `BDATE`, `ESSN,PNAME`. Note `ESSN,PNAME` works because whitespace tokenisation yields the tokens "ESSN," and "PNAME" — the comma stays attached, so no `,` separator is re-inserted.

Companion parseQuery (required to produce those info strings, /workspace/lab/QueryOptimize/main.cpp): split `query` on ' ' into tokens, pushing only when a space is seen (so the final ")" token is never pushed — harmless, since ")" is skipped anyway). Then walk tokens with `p = root`: `[` consumes tokens into `p->info` until `]`, concatenating with no separator; `(` followed by SELECT/PROJECTION creates that node as `p->left`, descends, and immediately consumes its `[ ... ]`; `(` followed by a bare relation name builds `TreeNode("JOIN")` with two `TreeNode("LEAF", name)` children (`it += 2` skips the literal "JOIN" token); `)` and `,` are skipped; any other bare token (in practice only the leading SELECT/PROJECTION) becomes `p->left` and `p` descends. The tree MUST be attached at `root->left` — main() dereferences `root->left` directly.

