Proposal B, verbatim — purely positional, zero schema knowledge. Reject the catalog/ownership machinery of A, C and D.

TreeNode *optimizeQuery(TreeNode *root, vector<string> info)
{
	TreeNode *p = root;
	if (p->type == "SELECT") {
		// split SELECT condtion by '&'
		vector<string> condition;
		string temp = "";
		for (size_t i = 0; i < p->info.length(); i++) {
			if (p->info[i] == '&') {
				condition.push_back(temp);
				temp = "";
			} else {
				temp += p->info[i];
			}
		}
		condition.push_back(temp);
		p = optimizeQuery(p->left, condition);   // SELECT node DISSOLVED
	} else if (p->type == "PROJECTION") {
		p->left = optimizeQuery(p->left, info);  // predicates pass straight through
	} else if (p->type == "JOIN") {
		string op = "SELECT";
		TreeNode *q = new TreeNode(op, info[0]); // conjunct 0 -> LEFT input
		q->left = p->left;
		p->left = q;
		if (info.size() > 1) {
			TreeNode *r = new TreeNode(op, info[1]); // conjunct 1 -> RIGHT input
			r->left = p->right;
			p->right = r;
		}
	}
	return p;
}

Mechanism:
- SPLIT: the SELECT's own condition is split on '&' only. No trimming is needed or wanted — parseQuery already produced space-free text, so trimming is a no-op here; adding a trim that also strips interior spaces would corrupt nothing but is pointless.
- ROUTE: the conjunct vector travels down as the `info` parameter. SELECT consumes itself and returns its optimized child (the node disappears from that position and is re-materialised lower down). PROJECTION forwards `info` unchanged and STAYS IN PLACE — no attribute-keeps check. JOIN materialises new SELECT nodes directly above its two inputs.
- ASSIGNMENT IS POSITIONAL, NOT SEMANTIC: conjunct[0] -> JOIN->left, conjunct[1] -> JOIN->right. There is no catalog; TreeNode carries only type and info. This is correct for these three queries only because the author wrote the conjuncts in relation order (ENAME before DNAME, EMPLOYEE before DEPARTMENT; single ESSN predicate -> left = WORKS_ON). Conjuncts beyond the second are silently dropped.
- RETURN VALUE: the (possibly new) subtree root, which is why main() does `root->left = optimizeQuery(root->left, vector<string>())`. Q1's optimized root becomes the JOIN; Q2's and Q3's are the PROJECTION.
- LEAF and empty-type nodes: no branch matches, so `p` is returned unchanged.

Three reference behaviours that are visible in stdout and must NOT be "improved":
(a) Q1's SELECT node vanishes entirely — the optimized root is JOIN, not a residual SELECT.
(b) Q3's SELECT is pushed THROUGH the PROJECTION even though ESSN appears in the projection list; the original SELECT node is discarded (leaked) and re-created below.
(c) Q3's single predicate lands on the JOIN's LEFT input (WORKS_ON) by position; PROJECT gets no SELECT above it.
Do not add attribute-ownership pushdown, do not insert PROJECTION nodes down the join branches, do not reorder join operands, do not merge co-located conjuncts with " & ", and do not free the discarded nodes.
